hi there steve kaufman here to talk  again about language learning from a  different perspective  we all know that motivation is the key  to language learning success  beyond anything else method school  whatever motivation is the driver of  success in language learning  now  in the english-speaking world  uh being monolingual  is the norm all right but there are many  societies but that's not the case  uh  in certain countries in europe for  example in singapore  in ethiopia  there are many places where being bi or  multilingual is the norm  and i don't think people in those places  are any different  so the difference is the motivation now  you can say that the circumstances in  those countries where many languages  coexist uh  that that's the reason but that in fact  is just a factor that contributes to  motivation all right  so and in the world so we have  monolingual people  typically in the english speaking world  in large countries you know japan russia  china  uh brazil  uh where people are less motivated to  learn  uh amongst the people who speak more  than one language you have  sort of people who speak two or three or  four  and we call them the multilingual people  bilingual multilingual and then you have  the so-called polyglots  who are motivated to go and learn more  and and also i think there's a  distinction between those who learn  languages for  professional or practical reasons and  those who learn languages as an  adventure in order to explore different  cultures travel meet people  get to know more of of the  people who are living at this time  with us and so on and so on so there's  these different distinctions however  it all begins at the beginning and  that's the gist of what i want to talk  about and that is  this idea that in language learning  you may not think that you're motivated  but  you know i encourage people who don't  think they're motivated to learn  languages to just do it  and i'm going to give some background to  that first of all there is an expression  that is attributed to aristotle  well begun is half done  so in other words just get started and  you'll be well on your way  and it reminds me  in my life how  just by committing myself to doing  something  this has brought all kinds of other  unintended or unforeseeable advantages  and i was reminded of that because i  received an email  from a swedish business associate  a person and his company we did business  for almost 20 years he and his wife  visited us here in palm springs  and he sent me a graph  of the sudden spike in north american  lumber prices in 1993 which is precisely  when  i contacted him in order to have wood  lumber supplied from sweden to japan  now how was i able to contact this  person well  back in nine two or three years earlier  in my young company because i created my  lumber company in 1987 and we had  different projects but one of our  projects was to develop  lumber supply from canada to europe and  so we hired a european lumber  grading expert to help us develop  product for europe and we were selling  uh lumber graded to european standards  in various markets in europe and of  course the grading expert we hired was  from sweden so i developed these  contacts in sweden  then when lumber prices in north america  spiked it was no longer possible to  export lumber from north america to  europe because their prices were lower  and north american prices were higher so  that the producing  mills in north america no longer wanted  to make  products for a lower priced market but  because i had initiated all of that  activity i realized that there now was a  different opportunity i could bring wood  from europe to japan and today my  company is importing wood from europe to  north america so  just by having committed myself to  developing  wood for the european market i developed  contacts which ultimately helped me  develop and maintain this other business  so you never know what's going to lead  to what uh the same has been true in in  my language learning activities uh i  you know i got interested in french i  ended up going to france because i  studied in france therefore i was  accepted into the canadian diplomatic  service i mean i could go on and on the  point is  if you do something if you commit to  doing something like nothing  everything in this world is a matter of  opportunities that are waiting to happen  and you are either going to  get out there and do things that cause  these opportunities to come your way or  you sit back and do nothing so  with languages again  uh you know i i follow on twitter people  like holy richards who's now in japan  and he sends these lovely pictures of  the countryside in shikoku and he  committed to learning japanese and now  he's traveling in japan and having a  wonderful  cultural culinary whatever tourist  experience  and so you never know right right now  i'm i'm learning arabic um  it's been two and a half months or so  not progressing too badly i'm not not  unhappy with my progress it's tough but  what is this going to lead to what kinds  of opportunities what kinds of people am  i going to meet what kind of travel what  am i going to discover about  you know  arabic and that whole culture and  civilization and and once i've learned  the arabic writing that'll lead me on to  farsi and discovering more about  uh you know middle east uh central asia  and so forth so  all i'm saying is there's a phenomenal  world out there that awaits people who  take on at first one new language  and possibly another new language  and whether you are learning for  practical reasons  or  as an adventurer as an explorer or both  you can expect  more wonderful things to happen to you  if you commit  take that first step down this path  and i think in my own case i started out  as a bit of an explorer exploring french  then i had some professional reasons to  learn chinese and japanese and then i  became a bit of an explorer and then  also  doing it for practical reasons because  it it helped me in my business  in europe so  just do it  well begun is half done  get out there get started and you'll be  i think amazed at all the good things  that will come your way so a bit of a  strange rant but those were the thoughts  that were crossing my mind this morning  thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

